      "user": "I lost my place during a presentation. Give me some advice.",
      "expected": {
        "mode": "DIRECT_HELP"
      },
      "output": {
        "message": "Here are three practical ways to handle losing your place: 1. **Take the pause on purpose:** Silence feels much longer to you than it does to the audience. A three- to five-second pause usually just looks like you are letting an idea settle. Take a breath and look down at your notes without rushing to fill the air.\n2. **Use a neutral bridge line:** You don't need to apologize or announce that you're lost. Simple lines buy time naturally, like *\"Let me make sure I give you the exact details on this next point,\"* or *\"Let's pull back to the main takeaway here.\"*\n3. **Summarize the last point you remember:** If you can't recall what comes next, restate what you just covered (*\"So, the core issue there was...\"*). Hearing yourself say the last concrete thought often triggers the next step in your outline. Are you looking to prepare recovery strategies for next time, or are you trying to shake off a presentation that just ended?",
